from mk import mk
mk("28uxHnQbulE","Wavestate Cinematic Pad in Minutes",
 "four-layer-wave-sequence-cinematic-pad-crossfade-vs-rhythmic",
 "Four-layer wave-sequenced cinematic pad: crossfaded seconds-based wave sequence, released keys layer, LFO-rate-modulated analog layer, tempo-locked rhythmic step layer with shape/ratchet/probability",
 "Build an evolving cinematic pad from four complementary layers: (1) a smooth wave sequence that crossfades between several string-like sources at seconds-based step times (~1 s) with slow attack/release, compression, tape delay and reverb; (2) a plain keys/piano layer with long release, gentle velocity-sensitive filter envelope and its own delay; (3) a synthetic layer sequencing between two different saw sources through a filter envelope, with a free-running LFO whose rate is itself modulated by a second slow free-running LFO (plus a touch of pitch deviation and panning from that LFO); (4) a tempo-locked (quarter-note) step layer with crossfade off, using saw-down/triangle step shapes on cutoff, double/triple ratchets at reduced probability and a tempo-synced filter LFO — then balance layers and put the busiest one on a macro.",
 ["Synth with ≥4 layers/voices per patch, a wave-sequence or step-sequence of oscillator sources (Web Audio: per-step source crossfade via GainNodes scheduled on AudioParam timelines, implementation note)","Filter + envelopes per layer, 2+ LFOs with free-run mode and LFO→LFO-rate routing","Compression, tape-style delay, reverb, phaser (optional)"],
 [("chords","midi","Sustained chords",True),("tempo","number","Project tempo for the rhythmic layer",False)],
 [("layered_pad","audio","Evolving four-layer cinematic pad")],
 ["Use original/free samples or generic waves in place of factory multisamples — no factory sample/preset names copied.","Presenter improvised from an init patch; values are approximate and by ear.","All four layers together can get busy — balance levels and use a macro on the rhythmic/analog layer."],
 [("Layer 1 (smooth wave sequence): choose 3–4 string-like sources moving from acoustic chamber/orchestral strings toward a synthetic string; set step time in seconds (~1 s per step) with crossfade on so each source melts into the next.","wave-sequence","Continuously morphing string bed"),
  ("Layer 1 envelope + FX: slower attack, some release, lengthen crossfade slightly; add compression, tape delay and master reverb (chorus not needed).","amp-env","Lush, slowly evolving string layer"),
  ("Layer 2 (keys): a single piano/keys source with a long, nice release; optionally lower cutoff with a release-shaped filter envelope, make it velocity-sensitive, add a different delay. A phaser was tried but softened the attack — compression worked better to give the keys impact over the strings.","keys-layer","Articulate keys line cutting through the pad"),
  ("Layer 3 (analog-style): wave-sequence between two different saw sources for variety; filter with filter envelope and tape delay.","analog-layer","Synthetic brass/string tone with subtle source changes"),
  ("Layer 3 motion: route a free-running LFO to the layer (e.g. filter/level), then use a second, very slow free-running LFO (the spare pan LFO) to modulate the first LFO's speed so it speeds up and slows down; use that slow LFO also for a tiny pitch deviation and for panning.","lfo-on-lfo-rate","Breathing modulation whose speed drifts over time"),
  ("Layer 4 (rhythmic): new wave sequence in tempo mode (quarter notes, speed up later) through synthetic/spectral and vocal-like sources; turn crossfade off so steps are audible.","step-sequence","Tempo-locked rhythmic source changes"),
  ("Shape the steps: give the step lane a saw-down shape (a pulse of 100 that decays to 0 = pseudo-envelope per step), lower cutoff and apply the step shape to cutoff; add double/triple ratchets on some steps and lower their probability so it isn't jarring; add a tempo-synced filter LFO and interesting delays.","step-shape","Plucky, sometimes-ratcheted pulses inside the pad"),
  ("Key-sync the rhythmic layer's steps so chords start in sync; optionally jump the last step up one or two octaves.","sync","Rhythmic layer locks to each chord start"),
  ("Balance: bring the busy analog layer down, map it (or the rhythmic layer) to a macro knob for real-time control.","mix","Layers sit together; density controllable live")],
 ["Layer 1 alone changes timbre smoothly with no audible steps; layer 4 alone changes audibly on the beat.","Layer 3's modulation rate is audibly not constant over ~10 s.","Ratchets don't fire on every bar when probability is reduced."],
 ["wave-sequence","layering","pad","lfo","step-sequencer","ratchet","probability","web-audio"])

mk("mBvUObtJTJI","Clouds (and clones) from the Ground Up: a reverbless explanation of a Granular icon",
 "granular-buffer-core-position-density-freeze-feedback-modes",
 "Granular buffer core from the ground up: grain size/shape/pitch, regular vs random density, triggered grains as a voice, position delay, freeze scrubbing, partial re-freeze, random pan, two feedback modes",
 "Understand and drive a buffer-based granular processor (the kind we need as a Web Audio module) with reverb and feedback turned down first: grains are tens-of-ms-to-1-s micro-samples plucked from a rolling 1–8 s input buffer; set size, shape (gate → triangle → Hann for overlap), pitch; density from noon (none) toward regular spacing one way and random spacing the other, or fire grains from a trigger with sequenced pitch to make a playable voice; use position as a buffer read point (= delay, newest at one end), freeze to scrub inside captured audio, gate the freeze with semi-random gates to partially overwrite and blend the buffer, mind lo-fi buffer modes (low sample rate → low-pass before input; mu-law 8-bit → keep input mid-level), random L/R grain pan for mono→stereo, and know that feedback is true input feedback when live but a separate delay/all-pass diffuser when frozen.",
 ["Granular processor with input buffer, freeze, position, size, shape/texture, pitch, density, trigger input (Web Audio: AudioWorklet ring buffer + windowed grain scheduler, implementation note)","A gate/trigger source and pitch sequencer/quantizer for the voice demo","Speech/radio/varied audio source for position demos"],
 [("input_audio","audio","Audio to capture into the granular buffer",True),("grain_triggers","trigger","Optional gates firing individual grains",False),("grain_pitch","cv","Optional quantized pitch per grain",False)],
 [("granular_output","audio","Grain stream: voice, scrub texture or evolving frozen bed (stereo with random pan)")],
 ["Start with internal feedback and reverb DOWN — they obscure what the granular core is doing; add external reverb later (internal reverb only affects the granular output).","Lo-fi buffer modes trade fidelity for time: 16 kHz sampling caps content at 8 kHz (low-pass the input to reduce aliasing); 8-bit mu-law favours mid levels — keep input around mid-scale on the meter.","Stereo mode halves record time; L and R are granularized separately only with internal reverb off and random pan at minimum.","Saved buffers are raw material, not presets — reloading won't reproduce a performance."],
 [("Turn internal feedback and reverb down. Feed a simple source (sine) and listen to individual grains.","setup","Discrete blips of the input"),
  ("Grain structure: size sets grain length (tens of ms → ~1 s); shape/texture morphs from no contour (gate) through triangle to Hann-like windows; use gate-like shapes for individual grains and Hann for overlapping grains; pitch shifts each grain up/down.","grain","Audible change in grain length, envelope smoothness and pitch"),
  ("Grain generation: density at noon = no grains; one direction = evenly spaced grains getting faster until they overlap; the other = randomly spaced grains until they overlap. Fine control near noon matters for counting individual grains.","density","Regular vs irregular grain streams"),
  ("Triggered voice: density at noon, feed clock-derived gates into the trigger input and quantized pitch per grain from a random/looping sequencer; send an occasional pulse into size to lengthen some notes.","trigger","Granular processor behaves like a playable synth voice with some long notes"),
  ("Position: switch to a varied source (speech/radio). Small grains + enough density reconstructs the input; moving position away from the newest end adds a growing delay (there's always a tiny conversion delay at minimum).","position","Position works as a read point/delay into the rolling buffer"),
  ("Freeze (fully wet): buffer stops updating; the same grain repeats at the current position; shrink size from a whole word to a tiny tone, then sweep position to scrub through captured audio (note: newest audio sits at one end, so 'forward' playback means moving position toward the older end).","freeze","Scrubbing inside a captured phrase; tiny grains become tones"),
  ("Evolving buffer: gate the freeze input with semi-random gates so it briefly unfreezes, writing a few seconds of new audio at the front while the rest of the buffer is kept.","freeze-gate","Buffer contents blend old and new material over time"),
  ("Post-processing: set wet/dry blend; use random L/R grain panning to turn a mono input into a stereo output; add reverb externally if preferred.","post","Stereo granular image without internal reverb wash"),
  ("Feedback: when live, it's true feedback of output into the buffer input (can run away — tame by modulating size/position); when frozen, feedback becomes a separate delay + all-pass diffusion that sounds reverb-like without altering the buffer. Watch for jumps when unfreezing.","feedback","Controlled feedback in both modes; no explosion on unfreeze")],
 ["With density at noon and no triggers, output is silent.","Frozen buffer + position sweep scrubs recognizable fragments of the source.","Random pan produces a stereo output from a mono input (L≠R).","Toggling freeze while feedback is up does not cause runaway level."],
 ["granular","freeze","buffer","density","grain-window","feedback","stereo","modular","web-audio"])

mk("Dazk-D79k5U","Mutable Instruments Beads FREEZE Explained – Frozen Textures & Tricks",
 "frozen-granular-buffer-as-playable-wavetable-voice",
 "Frozen granular buffer as a playable 'wavetable' voice: capture an evolving sound, freeze, scan time position, sequence pitch via v/oct for overlapping grain polyphony, random-modulate time/size",
 "Turn a granular processor's freeze into an instrument: capture an evolving sound into the buffer (lower-quality mode for ~10 s stereo / 20 s mono), freeze it, trigger seeds from gates, scan the time/position control through the capture like a homemade wavetable, sequence the frozen audio's pitch via v/oct so overlapping grains create a polyphonic illusion, use built-in random modulators on time and grain size, triangle grain window, reverb/delay, and for dark textures pitch the frozen capture down with high density and heavy reverb — the delta vs our granular-stack-freeze skill is pitched, sequenced playback of the frozen buffer.",
 ["Granular processor with freeze, time/position, size, shape, density, pitch (v/oct) input and seed/trigger input","Gate + pitch sequencer","Reverb/delay"],
 [("capture_source","audio","Evolving sound/arp to freeze",True),("gates","trigger","Gates for grain seeds",True),("pitch_seq","cv","v/oct pitch sequence",True)],
 [("frozen_voice","audio","Pitched, scannable frozen-buffer voice or dark texture")],
 ["Freeze captures the source at that moment; record what you want to play first.","Values (buffer times per quality mode) are from the presenter's module; other engines differ."],
 [("Run the granular processor fully wet; generate grains with the seed control; set mid size and a triangle grain shape (fades in and out cleanly); add reverb — integral to big ambient textures.","granular","Smooth grain stream from the input"),
  ("Patch a gate into the seed input and adjust density (grains per seed — it can reach audio rate).","density","Gated grain bursts of controllable density"),
  ("Select a lower-quality buffer mode to get more record time (presenter: ~10 s stereo / 20 s mono), play an evolving sound in, and freeze it.","freeze","Captured, static buffer"),
  ("Scan the time/position control through the frozen audio — like creating your own wavetable.","position","Distinct timbres at different scan positions"),
  ("Patch a pitch sequence into v/oct so the frozen buffer is played melodically; use the internal random modulators (attenurandomizers) on time and size; add reverb and delay. Overlapping grains give a polyphony-like overlap.","v-oct","Melodic, polyphonic-sounding frozen-buffer voice"),
  ("Try reversed grains for variation.","reverse","Reverse-grain texture"),
  ("Dark texture variant: capture a simple tone, pitch the frozen buffer down with density high and reverb high, keep slow random modulation on time so it scrolls and occasionally surfaces higher partials ('ominous wind chimes'); play root changes from a sequencer.","texture","Mostly droning dystopian bed with occasional high glints"),
  ("Optional: modulate freeze itself with gates to alternate between live grains and long held frozen notes.","freeze-gate","Held notes emerge and release")],
 ["Changing v/oct changes the pitch of the frozen material without changing the buffer.","Moving position audibly changes timbre (wavetable-like).","Pitch-down + high density yields a continuous drone, not discrete grains."],
 ["granular","freeze","wavetable","v-oct","texture","drone","modular","web-audio"])

mk("c8aQMp0vkRg","Make Noise DPO — The Ultimate Complex Oscillator?",
 "complex-oscillator-cross-fm-follow-sync-fold-voice",
 "Complex-oscillator voice: bidirectional cross-FM, sync + pitch-follow slew between two VCOs, osc-modulated wavefolder into an S&H-swept self-resonant filter",
 "Patch a West-Coast complex-oscillator voice from two VCOs: hard-wire exponential FM in both directions (osc1→osc2 and osc2→osc1) and dial sweet spots with both active, use soft/hard sync on osc1, add a 'follow' (osc1 slews to track osc2's v/oct at a set follow rate) for gliding harmonies, take osc2 through a wavefolder whose shape/angle are modulated by osc1 (mod bus), then split outputs: saw → multi-filter + chorus as bass, square → arpeggio with tiny vibrato into linear FM, folded output → filter at full resonance swept by sample-and-hold, into a clocked stereo delay.",
 ["Two oscillators with expo + linear FM between them, sync, a slew/'follow' path for pitch (Web Audio: two OscillatorNodes cross-feeding frequency AudioParams via GainNodes; AudioWorklet for sync, implementation note)","Wavefolder with shape/symmetry controls (WaveShaperNode)","Resonant filter, sample-and-hold, stereo delay, sequencer"],
 [("seq_pitch","cv","Pitch sequence into osc2 (and optionally osc1)",True),("clock","trigger","Clock for S&H and delay",False)],
 [("complex_voice","audio","Evolving FM/folded voice with bass, arp and resonant layer")],
 ["Presenter jams by ear; no numeric settings — 'dial in the sweet spots'.","Cross-FM in both directions at once gets unstable/chaotic quickly; small amounts first."],
 [("Exponential FM osc1→osc2: raise the amount and listen; then osc2→osc1 likewise; then use both simultaneously and find sweet spots (a little delay helps hear it).","cross-fm","Rich, then chaotic FM spectra"),
  ("Osc1 options: soft sync (lock) or hard sync to osc2 with a richer waveform; or switch osc1 to LFO mode as an internal modulator.","sync","Sync timbres / internal modulation"),
  ("Follow: send v/oct to osc2; set osc1 to follow osc2's pitch with a follow (slew) rate so osc1 glides after it; combine sync + follow, then move osc1's pitch to find harmonies/harmonics, or disable follow so osc1 drones on one note.","follow","Gliding second voice tracking the sequence"),
  ("Wavefolder output: lower the sine outputs, listen to the folder; adjust shape and angle (symmetry); bring up the mod bus so osc1 modulates the folder.","wavefolder","Animated folded timbres"),
  ("Bass: osc1 saw sequenced separately → analog filter → stereo chorus.","bass","Big fat bassline"),
  ("Arp: osc2 square plays an arpeggiated sequence with a tiny vibrato into the linear FM input.","arp","Arpeggio with subtle pitch life"),
  ("Resonant layer: folded output → filter with resonance fully up, cutoff modulated by sample-and-hold; blend in some FM on the folder.","s&h-filter","Folded tone plus singing sine from the self-resonant filter, randomly stepping"),
  ("Send everything through a clocked stereo delay; record — results are hard to recreate.","fx","Epic evolving patch printed")],
 ["Both FM directions active produces audibly different spectra from either alone.","With follow on, osc1 audibly glides after each osc2 pitch change.","Filter at full resonance with S&H shows stepped sine pitches."],
 ["complex-oscillator","fm","cross-modulation","sync","wavefolder","sample-and-hold","west-coast","web-audio"])
